(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["physiology-exam-1-cns-function-quiz"]={"title":"Central Nervous System Function — Physiology Exam 1","category":"Physiology","questions":[{"q":"Where are the cell bodies of preganglionic and postganglionic autonomic neurons located?","choices":["Preganglionic cell bodies are located in the dorsal root ganglia outside the CNS, with a heavily myelinated axon projecting directly to the visceral effector","Preganglionic cell body in the brainstem or spinal cord, thinly myelinated axon to the ganglion; postganglionic cell body in the autonomic ganglion, unmyelinated axon to the visceral effector","Both preganglionic and postganglionic cell bodies are located together within the brainstem or spinal cord, with only their combined axon terminating in the ganglion","Postganglionic cell bodies are located in the brainstem or spinal cord, with an unmyelinated axon projecting directly to the visceral effector cell"],"answer":1,"exp":"The preganglionic neuron's cell body is located in the brainstem or spinal cord, with a thinly myelinated axon projecting to an autonomic ganglion; the postganglionic neuron's cell body is located within that ganglion, with an unmyelinated axon projecting to the visceral effector cell. — Lecture 5 - Nervous System - Central Nervous System Function-2.pptx, Slide 4"},{"q":"Where are sympathetic ganglia located compared to parasympathetic ganglia, and what does this mean for fiber length?","choices":["Sympathetic ganglia are close to the spinal cord (short preganglionic, long postganglionic fibers); parasympathetic ganglia are in the effector tissue (long preganglionic, short postganglionic fibers)","Parasympathetic ganglia are located close to the spinal cord, giving long postganglionic and short preganglionic fibers, the reverse of sympathetic organization","Sympathetic ganglia are located in the effector tissue itself, giving short postganglionic and long preganglionic fibers similar to parasympathetic organization","Both ganglia types are located immediately adjacent to the spinal cord, producing uniformly short preganglionic and long postganglionic fibers in both divisions"],"answer":0,"exp":"Sympathetic ganglia are located close to the spinal cord, giving short preganglionic and long postganglionic fibers, while parasympathetic ganglia are located in the effector tissues themselves, giving long preganglionic and short postganglionic fibers. — Lecture 5 - Nervous System - Central Nervous System Function-2.pptx, Slide 5"},{"q":"Which cranial nerve carries parasympathetic fibers to the pupillary sphincter and ciliary muscle?","choices":["CN X (vagus)","CN VII (facial)","CN IX (glossopharyngeal)","CN III (oculomotor)"],"answer":3,"exp":"Parasympathetic fibers originate from cranial nerves III, VII, IX, and X plus the sacral spinal cord: CN III carries fibers to the pupillary sphincter/ciliary muscle, CN VII to nasal/lacrimal/submandibular glands, CN IX to the parotid gland, and CN X to visceral organs. — Lecture 5 - Nervous System - Central Nervous System Function-2.pptx, Slide 6"},{"q":"What defines an \"adrenergic\" nerve versus a \"cholinergic\" nerve?","choices":["Both terms refer to the same type of nerve","Adrenergic nerves release norepinephrine; cholinergic nerves release acetylcholine","Adrenergic nerves are only found in the parasympathetic system","Adrenergic nerves release acetylcholine; cholinergic nerves release norepinephrine"],"answer":1,"exp":"Sympathetic nerves release norepinephrine at their endings and are called adrenergic nerves; parasympathetic nerves release acetylcholine at their endings and are called cholinergic nerves. — Lecture 5 - Nervous System - Central Nervous System Function-2.pptx, Slide 7"},{"q":"What neurotransmitter do ALL preganglionic autonomic fibers (both sympathetic and parasympathetic) release?","choices":["Dopamine","Epinephrine","Norepinephrine","Acetylcholine"],"answer":3,"exp":"All preganglionic sympathetic and parasympathetic nerve fibers release acetylcholine, regardless of what their corresponding postganglionic fibers release. — Lecture 5 - Nervous System - Central Nervous System Function-2.pptx, Slide 8"},{"q":"What effect does activation of beta-1 adrenergic receptors have on the heart, and what does beta-2 activation cause?","choices":["Beta-1 increases heart rate and contractility; beta-2 causes bronchial dilation, vasodilation in skeletal muscle, calorigenesis, and glycogenolysis","Beta-1 causes bronchial dilation and vasodilation in skeletal muscle; beta-2 increases heart rate and contractility, opposite the true assignment","Both receptors produce identical vasoconstriction and bronchoconstriction, mediated through the same alpha-adrenergic signaling cascade","Neither receptor affects the heart or lungs; both act exclusively on gastrointestinal smooth muscle motility"],"answer":0,"exp":"Beta-1 receptor activation increases heart rate and contractility; beta-2 receptor activation causes bronchial dilation, dilation of blood vessels in skeletal muscle, calorigenesis, and glycogenolysis. Alpha receptors, by contrast, are located on blood vessels and cause vasoconstriction. — Lecture 5 - Nervous System - Central Nervous System Function-2.pptx, Slide 9"},{"q":"Where are nicotinic acetylcholine receptors found, versus muscarinic receptors?","choices":["Muscarinic receptors are ligand-gated ion channels structurally identical to nicotinic receptors, and both types are found exclusively at the skeletal neuromuscular junction","Nicotinic and muscarinic receptors are found in identical locations, both concentrated at autonomic ganglia and on parasympathetic effector organs alike","Muscarinic receptors are only found at the skeletal neuromuscular junction, where they mediate fast excitatory transmission via ligand-gated channels","Nicotinic receptors are found at synapses between pre- and postganglionic neurons and at the neuromuscular junction; muscarinic receptors are found on effector cells stimulated by postganglionic parasympathetic fibers"],"answer":3,"exp":"Nicotinic receptors (ligand-gated ion channels) are found in autonomic ganglia (between pre- and postganglionic neurons) and at the skeletal muscle neuromuscular junction; muscarinic receptors (G protein-coupled, 5 subtypes M1-M5) are found on effector cells stimulated by postganglionic parasympathetic fibers. — Lecture 5 - Nervous System - Central Nervous System Function-2.pptx, Slide 10"},{"q":"Where do parasympathetic preganglionic fibers typically synapse, compared to sympathetic preganglionic fibers?","choices":["Both PNS and SNS fibers synapse exclusively within the sympathetic chain, regardless of division, before a long postganglionic fiber reaches the target organ","PNS fibers synapse in the target organ (except CN III, VII, IX); SNS fibers synapse outside the target organ in the sympathetic chain/ganglion (except the adrenal medulla)","SNS fibers always synapse within the target organ itself (except the adrenal medulla), the exact reverse of the typical parasympathetic pattern","PNS fibers never synapse near the target organ, instead synapsing far away in the sympathetic chain alongside sympathetic preganglionic fibers"],"answer":1,"exp":"Parasympathetic preganglionic fibers typically synapse within the target organ (with the exception of CN III, VII, and IX), while sympathetic preganglionic fibers synapse outside the target organ in the sympathetic chain or a ganglion (with the exception of the adrenal medulla). — Lecture 5 - Nervous System - Central Nervous System Function-2.pptx, Slide 11"},{"q":"How does norepinephrine's receptor selectivity differ from epinephrine's?","choices":["NE activates almost exclusively beta-2 receptors, producing bronchodilation and skeletal muscle vasodilation as its primary effect","Neither hormone shows meaningful selectivity for alpha receptors; both act almost exclusively through beta-1 and beta-2 pathways","NE mostly activates alpha receptors (with a small effect on beta); Epi activates alpha and beta receptors about equally","NE activates alpha and beta receptors about equally; Epi mostly activates alpha receptors with only a small effect on beta receptors"],"answer":2,"exp":"Norepinephrine mostly activates alpha receptors (with a small effect on beta receptors), while epinephrine activates alpha and beta receptors about equally. — Lecture 5 - Nervous System - Central Nervous System Function-2.pptx, Slide 13"},{"q":"What is albuterol, and what is it used for clinically?","choices":["A selective alpha-1 agonist used clinically to raise blood pressure or to shrink swollen nasal mucous membranes in decongestants","A selective beta-2 agonist, used as a short-acting bronchodilator for COPD, asthma, and emphysema","A non-selective beta agonist used clinically to treat bradycardia, in contrast to selective beta-2 or beta-1 agents used for other indications","A non-selective alpha blocker used clinically to treat hypertension by preventing vasoconstriction at both alpha-1 and alpha-2 receptors"],"answer":1,"exp":"Albuterol is a selective beta-2 agonist used as a short-acting bronchodilator to treat COPD, asthma, and emphysema, in contrast to isoproterenol (non-selective beta agonist, used for bradycardia) and phenylephrine (selective alpha-1 agonist, used to raise BP or shrink nasal blood vessels). — Lecture 5 - Nervous System - Central Nervous System Function-2.pptx, Slide 13"},{"q":"What percentage of catecholamines released by the adrenal medulla is epinephrine versus norepinephrine, and how does the duration of circulating catecholamine effects compare to direct sympathetic stimulation?","choices":["80% Epi, 20% NE; effects last 5-10x longer (2-4 minutes) than direct sympathetic stimulation","50% Epi, 50% NE; circulating effects last only about as long as direct sympathetic nerve stimulation itself","20% Epi, 80% NE; effects last only half as long (30-60 seconds) as direct sympathetic stimulation","100% Epi, 0% NE; effects last 5-10x longer (2-4 minutes) than direct sympathetic stimulation"],"answer":0,"exp":"The adrenal medulla releases about 80% epinephrine and 20% norepinephrine into the blood; circulating catecholamine effects last 5-10 times longer (2-4 minutes) than the effects of direct sympathetic nerve stimulation. — Lecture 5 - Nervous System - Central Nervous System Function-2.pptx, Slide 17"},{"q":"Why does epinephrine have a greater effect on stimulating the heart, while norepinephrine has a greater effect on constricting blood vessels?","choices":["Norepinephrine stimulates beta receptors more strongly than epinephrine, giving it the greater effect on the heart, while epinephrine stimulates alpha receptors more strongly, giving it the greater effect constricting vessels -- the reverse of the true relationship","Epinephrine acts exclusively on cardiac beta-1 receptors with no effect anywhere else; norepinephrine acts exclusively on vascular alpha receptors with no effect on the heart or metabolism at all","Epinephrine stimulates beta receptors more (heart); norepinephrine stimulates alpha receptors more (vasoconstriction, increasing TPR and arterial pressure)","Both hormones have identical receptor selectivity, stimulating alpha and beta receptors in exactly the same proportion and producing indistinguishable cardiovascular effects"],"answer":2,"exp":"Epinephrine has a greater effect stimulating the heart due to greater beta receptor stimulation, while norepinephrine has a greater effect constricting blood vessels due to greater alpha receptor stimulation (increasing total peripheral resistance and arterial pressure); epinephrine also increases metabolic rate 5-10x more than norepinephrine. — Lecture 5 - Nervous System - Central Nervous System Function-2.pptx, Slide 17"},{"q":"What is autonomic \"tone,\" and what is its functional significance?","choices":["The maximal possible activity of a system, a fixed ceiling reached only during mass discharge that cannot be exceeded regardless of further sympathetic or parasympathetic stimulation","The basal rate of activity present only in the parasympathetic system, since the sympathetic system has no baseline activity and only fires during acute stress","The basal (background) rate of activity of the sympathetic or parasympathetic system, which allows activity to increase or decrease from that baseline (e.g., sympathetic tone causes about 50% vasoconstriction at baseline)","A historical term with no real physiological basis, since neither division maintains any baseline activity between discrete reflex responses"],"answer":2,"exp":"Autonomic tone is the basal rate of activity of each system; this background activity allows for an increase OR decrease in activity by a single system — sympathetic tone normally causes about 50% vasoconstriction, and parasympathetic tone provides background GI activity. — Lecture 5 - Nervous System - Central Nervous System Function-2.pptx, Slide 22"},{"q":"What triggers mass discharge of the sympathetic nervous system (the \"fight-or-flight\" reaction), and what happens to blood flow to the GI tract?","choices":["It is triggered by parasympathetic activation during rest and digestion; blood flow to the GI tract increases while flow to skeletal muscle decreases correspondingly","Activation of the hypothalamus by fright or severe pain; blood flow to the GI tract decreases while flow to active muscles increases","It is triggered only by exposure to cold temperatures, activating brown fat thermogenesis while leaving GI and muscle blood flow essentially unchanged","Mass discharge has no effect on GI blood flow, redirecting cardiac output solely toward the skin and kidneys instead of active skeletal muscle"],"answer":1,"exp":"Mass discharge of the SNS occurs when the hypothalamus is activated by fright or severe pain (the fight-or-flight reaction), causing increased blood flow to active muscles and decreased blood flow to the GI tract and kidneys, along with increased heart rate, metabolism, blood glucose, and muscle strength. — Lecture 5 - Nervous System - Central Nervous System Function-2.pptx, Slide 23"},{"q":"What is the mechanism of cholinesterase inhibitor drugs like neostigmine and pyridostigmine?","choices":["They deplete presynaptic acetylcholine stores by blocking its synthesis","They potentiate the effect of acetylcholine by inhibiting its breakdown","They stimulate nicotinic receptors directly, mimicking acetylcholine itself","They block acetylcholine receptors directly, preventing its binding"],"answer":1,"exp":"Cholinesterase inhibitors (neostigmine, pyridostigmine, ambenonium) potentiate the effect of acetylcholine by inhibiting the enzyme that normally breaks it down, prolonging its action at the synapse. — Lecture 5 - Nervous System - Central Nervous System Function-2.pptx, Slide 26"},{"q":"What is the mechanism of antimuscarinic drugs like atropine and scopolamine?","choices":["They selectively block nicotinic receptors at autonomic ganglia rather than muscarinic receptors on effector cells","They increase acetylcholine synthesis and release from postganglionic parasympathetic terminals","They stimulate muscarinic receptors, mimicking and enhancing the effect of acetylcholine on effector cells","They block the effect of acetylcholine on effector cells (at muscarinic receptors)"],"answer":3,"exp":"Antimuscarinic drugs such as atropine and scopolamine block the effect of acetylcholine on effector cells at muscarinic receptors. — Lecture 5 - Nervous System - Central Nervous System Function-2.pptx, Slide 26"},{"q":"What is the mechanism of ganglionic blockers like hexamethonium?","choices":["They enhance transmission between pre- and postganglionic neurons by prolonging nicotinic receptor activation at the ganglionic synapse","They block transmission at the autonomic ganglia (between pre- and postganglionic neurons)","They selectively block beta-1 receptors on the heart, without affecting transmission at the autonomic ganglia themselves","They increase norepinephrine release from postganglionic terminals by promoting vesicular exocytosis at the neuroeffector junction"],"answer":1,"exp":"Ganglionic blockers like hexamethonium block transmission at the autonomic ganglia, between the pre- and postganglionic neurons, affecting both sympathetic and parasympathetic pathways. — Lecture 5 - Nervous System - Central Nervous System Function-2.pptx, Slide 25"},{"q":"What are the respective effects of sympathetic and parasympathetic stimulation on the eye?","choices":["Neither division has any effect on pupil diameter, which is controlled entirely by ambient light reflexes at the retina","Sympathetic causes pupillary dilation; parasympathetic causes pupillary constriction and lens accommodation for near vision","Both divisions cause pupillary dilation, differing only in the speed and duration of the dilation response produced","Sympathetic causes lens accommodation for near vision; parasympathetic causes pupillary dilation for distance vision"],"answer":1,"exp":"Sympathetic stimulation causes pupillary dilation, while parasympathetic stimulation causes pupillary constriction and accommodation (focusing) of the lens for near vision. — Lecture 5 - Nervous System - Central Nervous System Function-2.pptx, Slide 19"},{"q":"Which autonomic division stimulates the nasal, lacrimal, salivary, and GI glands, and which stimulates sweat glands?","choices":["Parasympathetic stimulates nasal/lacrimal/salivary/GI glands; sympathetic stimulates sweat glands","Both divisions stimulate all gland types equally","Sympathetic stimulates nasal/lacrimal/salivary/GI glands; parasympathetic stimulates sweat glands","Neither division affects glandular secretion"],"answer":0,"exp":"Parasympathetic stimulation activates the nasal, lacrimal, salivary, and GI glands, while sympathetic stimulation activates sweat glands — an exception to the general pattern since sweat glands are sympathetically (cholinergically) innervated. — Lecture 5 - Nervous System - Central Nervous System Function-2.pptx, Slide 19"},{"q":"What are the respective effects of sympathetic and parasympathetic stimulation on heart rate and blood vessels?","choices":["Both divisions produce identical increases in heart rate and identical peripheral vasoconstriction, differing only in which cranial or spinal segments carry the outgoing signal to the heart","Sympathetic increases heart rate and causes vasoconstriction; parasympathetic decreases heart rate and causes some vasodilation","Parasympathetic stimulation has no measurable effect on heart rate or blood vessels, acting instead exclusively on glandular secretion and pupil size","Sympathetic stimulation decreases heart rate and causes vasodilation; parasympathetic stimulation causes vasoconstriction with little effect on rate"],"answer":1,"exp":"Sympathetic stimulation increases heart rate and contractility and causes vasoconstriction, while parasympathetic stimulation decreases heart rate and causes some vasodilation; sympathetic stimulation has very little direct effect on GI activity, which is primarily driven by parasympathetic stimulation. — Lecture 5 - Nervous System - Central Nervous System Function-2.pptx, Slide 20"},{"q":"From what amino acid are norepinephrine and epinephrine synthesized?","choices":["Glutamate (via GABA and succinate semialdehyde intermediates)","Tyrosine (via DOPA and dopamine intermediates)","Tryptophan (via 5-hydroxytryptophan and serotonin intermediates)","Glycine (via serine and threonine intermediates)"],"answer":1,"exp":"Norepinephrine and epinephrine are synthesized from the amino acid tyrosine, via the pathway tyrosine → DOPA → dopamine → norepinephrine → (epinephrine). — Lecture 5 - Nervous System - Central Nervous System Function-2.pptx, Slide 8"},{"q":"From what two components is acetylcholine formed?","choices":["Glutamate and acetyl-CoA","GABA and choline","Acetyl-CoA and choline","Tyrosine and choline"],"answer":2,"exp":"Acetylcholine is formed from a combination of acetyl-CoA and choline. — Lecture 5 - Nervous System - Central Nervous System Function-2.pptx, Slide 8"},{"q":"Which of the following is NOT among the visceral functions the ANS helps control?","choices":["Voluntary skeletal muscle contraction for walking","GI motility, secretions, and enzyme release throughout the tract","Arterial pressure regulation and heart rate control","Urinary bladder emptying and sweat gland secretion"],"answer":0,"exp":"The ANS controls most visceral functions (arterial pressure, heart rate/contractility, GI motility/secretions, bladder emptying, sweating, body temperature, vascular tone, airways), but voluntary skeletal muscle contraction (e.g., for walking) is controlled by the somatic motor system, not the ANS. — Lecture 5 - Nervous System - Central Nervous System Function-2.pptx, Slide 3"},{"q":"What poison activates muscarinic receptors, giving them their name?","choices":["Muscarine, a toadstool poison","Tetrodotoxin, a pufferfish toxin","Batrachotoxin, a poison dart frog toxin","Nicotine, a tobacco plant alkaloid"],"answer":0,"exp":"Muscarinic receptors are named for their activation by muscarine, a toadstool poison, while nicotinic receptors are named for their activation by nicotine. — Lecture 5 - Nervous System - Central Nervous System Function-2.pptx, Slide 12"},{"q":"What is the difference between a receptor agonist and a receptor antagonist?","choices":["An agonist blocks a biological response by binding to and blocking the receptor; an antagonist initiates a physiological response upon combining with its receptor","They are synonymous terms both describing any substance that binds a receptor and always produces the identical physiological response upon binding","Agonists and antagonists both always increase receptor activity, differing only in how strongly they bind and how long the response lasts","An agonist initiates a physiological response when it combines with its receptor; an antagonist blocks a biological response by binding to and blocking the receptor"],"answer":3,"exp":"A receptor agonist is a substance that initiates a physiological response when it combines with its receptor; a receptor antagonist is a substance that blocks a biological response by binding to and blocking the receptor. — Lecture 5 - Nervous System - Central Nervous System Function-2.pptx, Slide 13"},{"q":"Which effector tissues are exceptions to the rule that postganglionic sympathetic nerves release norepinephrine?","choices":["The heart and lungs, whose postganglionic sympathetic fibers release dopamine instead of norepinephrine","Only the adrenal medulla, whose chromaffin cells release acetylcholine directly into the bloodstream instead of norepinephrine","There are no exceptions -- every postganglionic sympathetic fiber releases norepinephrine onto its effector tissue without exception","Sweat glands, piloerector muscles, and select blood vessels (which are cholinergic)"],"answer":3,"exp":"Almost all postganglionic sympathetic nerves release norepinephrine, except for those innervating sweat glands, piloerector muscles, and select blood vessels, which release acetylcholine instead (sympathetic-cholinergic fibers). — Lecture 5 - Nervous System - Central Nervous System Function-2.pptx, Slide 7"},{"q":"What class of drug are phentolamine and phenoxybenzamine, and what do they block?","choices":["Alpha blockers, blocking alpha receptors","Ganglionic blockers, blocking autonomic ganglia","Cholinesterase inhibitors","Beta blockers, blocking beta receptors"],"answer":0,"exp":"Phentolamine and phenoxybenzamine are alpha blockers, blocking alpha adrenergic receptors, in contrast to beta blockers like propranolol (blocks beta-1 and beta-2) and metoprolol (blocks beta-1 selectively). — Lecture 5 - Nervous System - Central Nervous System Function-2.pptx, Slide 25"},{"q":"What is the key difference between propranolol and metoprolol as beta blockers?","choices":["Metoprolol blocks both beta-1 and beta-2 receptors non-selectively; propranolol selectively blocks only beta-1 receptors","Neither drug blocks any adrenergic receptor; both act instead by depleting presynaptic norepinephrine storage vesicles","Both drugs have identical non-selective activity, blocking beta-1 and beta-2 receptors equally with no clinical distinction","Propranolol blocks both beta-1 and beta-2 receptors; metoprolol selectively blocks beta-1 receptors"],"answer":3,"exp":"Propranolol is a non-selective beta blocker (blocking both beta-1 and beta-2 receptors), while metoprolol selectively blocks beta-1 receptors. — Lecture 5 - Nervous System - Central Nervous System Function-2.pptx, Slide 25"},{"q":"How do drugs like ephedrine, tyramine, and amphetamine act on the sympathetic nervous system?","choices":["They permanently destroy norepinephrine storage vesicles in the presynaptic terminal, depleting the neuron's capacity to release transmitter over time","They act indirectly by increasing the release of norepinephrine from its storage terminals","They have no effect on the sympathetic nervous system, acting instead exclusively as reuptake inhibitors at serotonergic synapses in the CNS","They act directly by binding and activating alpha and beta receptors on the postsynaptic membrane, without involving norepinephrine release at all"],"answer":1,"exp":"Ephedrine, tyramine, and amphetamine act indirectly on the sympathetic nervous system by increasing the release of norepinephrine from its storage terminals, rather than directly binding adrenergic receptors themselves. — Lecture 5 - Nervous System - Central Nervous System Function-2.pptx, Slide 24"},{"q":"How do reserpine and guanethidine differ in their mechanism of blocking sympathetic effects?","choices":["Reserpine blocks synthesis and storage of norepinephrine; guanethidine blocks its release from the nerve terminal","Neither drug affects norepinephrine directly; both instead work by degrading acetylcholine at the postganglionic synapse","Reserpine and guanethidine are both ganglionic blockers that prevent transmission between pre- and postganglionic sympathetic neurons","Both drugs block postsynaptic alpha receptors directly, preventing norepinephrine from binding regardless of how much is released"],"answer":0,"exp":"Reserpine blocks the synthesis and storage of norepinephrine, while guanethidine blocks its release from the nerve terminal — both reduce sympathetic effects but through different steps in the norepinephrine life cycle. — Lecture 5 - Nervous System - Central Nervous System Function-2.pptx, Slide 25"}]};
